# Feature Request Workflow

When implementing a feature request, treat tests as part of the deliverable—not optional follow-up.

## Process

1. **Clarify** — Restate the feature, acceptance criteria, and out-of-scope items before coding.
2. **Plan** — List the files/modules to touch and the unit + e2e cases that will prove the behavior.
3. **Implement** — Make minimal, focused changes that match existing project patterns.
4. **Test** — Ship unit and e2e coverage with the feature; do not mark work done until both pass.
5. **Validate** — Run the relevant suites and fix failures before finishing.

## Required Tests

Every feature must include:

| Layer | Tool | Location | What to cover |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Unit | Vitest | Colocated `*.test.ts` next to the module (or under the same feature folder) | Pure logic, parsers, schemas, filters, edge cases |
| E2E | Playwright | `tests/e2e/*.spec.ts` | User-visible flows through the UI/routes |

- Map each acceptance criterion to at least one failing-then-passing test.
- Prefer testing behavior users care about over implementation details.
- Extend existing suites when the feature extends an existing flow; add new files when the surface is new.
- Bug fixes that change behavior need regression tests at the appropriate layer(s).

## Commands

```powershell
pnpm test:unit
pnpm test:e2e
```

Run only the affected specs when iterating; run the full relevant suites before considering the feature complete.

## Definition of Done

A feature request is complete only when:

- [ ] Implementation matches the agreed acceptance criteria
- [ ] Unit tests cover the new/changed logic
- [ ] E2E tests cover the new/changed user flow
- [ ] `pnpm test:unit` and `pnpm test:e2e` (or the scoped suites for that feature) pass
- [ ] No unrelated refactors or drive-by cleanups were introduced

## Anti-Patterns

- Shipping UI or API changes without tests
- Only adding unit tests when the feature has a user-facing flow (or only e2e when logic belongs in unit tests)
- Claiming done while tests are skipped, failing, or left “for later”
- Broad rewrites unrelated to the feature in order to “make testing easier”
